example.paragraph:
Let $\mathbf{u}_{1} = \mspace{-180mu}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      5 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{u}_{2} = \mspace{-180mu}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\textbf{y} = \mspace{-180mu}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      2 \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Observe that $\left\{ \mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2} \right\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $W = \text{Span}\left\{ \mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2} \right\}$. Write $\textbf{y}$ as the sum of a vector in $W$ and a vector orthogonal to $W$.

If $\mathbf{u}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      5 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{u}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{y} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      2 \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $W = \text{Span~}\left\{ \mathbf{u}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{u}_{2} \right\}$, as in Example 2, then the closest point in $W$ to $\textbf{y}$ is

content.paragraph:
Let $W = \text{span}\left\{ \textbf{w} \right\}$. Choose a basis $\left\{ \textbf{v}_{1}\text{,}\textbf{v}_{2}\text{,}\ldots\text{,}\textbf{v}_{n - 1} \right\}$ for $W^{\bot}$. Create the matrix $B = \begin{bmatrix}
      {\textbf{v}_{1}}^{T} \\
      {\textbf{v}_{2}}^{T} \\
       \vdots \\
      {\textbf{v}_{n - 1}}^{T}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Notice $B\textbf{u} = \textbf{0}$ if and only if $\textbf{u}$ is orthogonal to a set of basis vectors for $W^{\bot}$, which happens if and only if $\textbf{u}$ is in $W$. Set $M = B^{T}B$. Then $\textbf{u}^{T}M\textbf{u} = \textbf{u}^{T}B^{T}B\textbf{u} = {(B\textbf{u})}^{T}B\textbf{u}$. By Theorem 1, ${(B\textbf{u})}^{T}B\textbf{u} = 0$ if and only if $B\textbf{u} = 0$, and hence $\textbf{u}^{T}M\textbf{u} = 0$ if and only if $\textbf{u} \in W$. But there are only two vectors in $W$ consisting of zeros and ones: $1\textbf{w} = \textbf{w}$ and $0\textbf{w} = \textbf{0}$. Thus we can conclude that if $\textbf{u}^{T}M\textbf{u} = 0$, but $\textbf{u}^{T}\textbf{u} \neq 0$, then $\textbf{u} = \textbf{w}$. See Figure 6.

